{
  "$comment": "urlencoded body escaper case table, shared byte-for-byte by the three engines that build an\napplication/x-www-form-urlencoded request body: openbindings-go/formats/openapi,\nopenapi-client/go, and openapi-client/typescript/src (which openbindings-ts/packages/openapi\nre-exports). Every expectation is derived from upstream authority text, never from an engine.\n\nThe OAS assigns a DIFFERENT percent-encoding specification to each of the two lanes, and the\ntwo lanes are therefore SUPPOSED to disagree about the space character. From the normatively-\ncited-standards table in OAS 3.0.4 / 3.1.1 Appendix E.3 (E.4 in 3.1.2):\n  [RFC6570]              style-based serialization   percent-encoding [RFC3986]\n                         notes: 'does not use + for form-urlencoded'\n  [RFC1866] Section 8.2.1 content-based serialization percent-encoding [RFC1738]\nand, immediately after it: 'Style-based serialization is used ... in the Encoding Object when\nat least one of style, explode, or allowReserved is present', while 'Content-based\nserialization ... [is used] with the Encoding Object based on the contentType field when the\nfields style, explode, and allowReserved are absent.'\n\nSTYLE lane  = RFC 6570 form expansion, allowReserved absent: literals are RFC 3986's unreserved\n              set (ALPHA / DIGIT / '-' / '.' / '_' / '~'); every other byte is uppercase %HH;\n              a space is %20.\nCONTENT lane = RFC 1866 Section 8.2.1 ('space characters are replaced by `+`'), with the literal\n              set fixed to the WHATWG form-urlencoded serializer set (ALPHA / DIGIT / '*' / '-'\n              / '.' / '_') on the authority of OAS Appendix E.3.2's SHOULD for browser\n              compatibility; that output is also a subset of what RFC 1738 permits unencoded,\n              satisfying E.3.1's SHOULD. Which member of the authority-permitted set the engines\n              pick is the IMPLEMENTATIONS' convention, not the binding specification's.\n\nLane selection is the OAS's own predicate, not a project invention, with one edition branch:\nOAS 3.0.0-3.0.3 apply the Encoding Object's form/explode defaults to every urlencoded property,\nso on that line BOTH declaration shapes take the style lane. The 3.0-line rows exist to prove\nthat no property moves lanes when the content lane's escaper changes.\n\nEach case declares one urlencoded request body with exactly one property; `expect` is the whole\nemitted body, which all three engines must produce byte for byte.",
